<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Province of West Prussia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was a province of the Kingdom of Prussia formed from most of Royal Prussia, which Prussia gained from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition</a> of 1772; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> followed in 1793. Its capital was Danzig, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> most of the province went to Poland under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-prussia.jpg" alt="West Prussia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Provinz_Westpreu%C3%9Fen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The province lay on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> near its mouth at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and on the lands to the west and east. In the west it bordered the easternmost part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and lay between the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, a region of the Baltic Uplands with the Tuchola Forest and the Kashubian Lake District, where the Turmberg reached 329 metres. In the southeast the river crossed a flat plain and the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Culmerland</a>, with the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Culm</a>, Thorn and Graudenz. Danzig was the largest city of the province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> took Danzig and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> in 1308, and German-speaking settlers were encouraged to move in. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a>, which ended the Thirteen Years' War, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> gained most of the region, which became Royal Prussia and kept its own legislature, the Prussian Estates, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick II of Prussia</a> incorporated the annexed lands into the new Province of West Prussia in 1773, giving Prussia a land link between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. German-speaking Protestants made up 54% of the annexed area. Danzig and Thorn were added in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> of 1793. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a> of 1807 the region around Thorn went to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and Danzig became a free city; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> returned them. West Prussia was joined with East Prussia as the Province of Prussia in 1829, and the two were separated again in 1878 within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> the authorities pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> and settlement policies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulturkampf">Kulturkampf</a> against Catholics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1910 counted 1,703,474 inhabitants, of whom 64.5% were German and 35.5% Polish; a Polish author argued in 1918 that the Polish share was nearer 42.5%.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> divided the province into four areas: the Free City of Danzig under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, the eastern districts as the administrative district of West Prussia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, the westernmost districts as part of Posen-West Prussia from 1922, and a central region given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as the Pomeranian Voivodeship, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-corridor">Polish Corridor</a>. In a plebiscite in 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a> area voted by 92.4% to stay with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. After the invasion of Poland in 1939 the region became part of the Reichsgau Danzig-West Prussia, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were expelled, enslaved or killed. In 1945 it was restored to Poland, and most of the remaining Germans were expelled.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Prussia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its territory, however, bore most of Germany's losses under the Treaty of Versailles: Eupen-Malmedy, the Hultschiner Ländchen, Danzig, the Memel Territory and much of Posen and <b>West Prussia</b>, leaving East Prussia cut off by the Polish Corridor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On 18 January 1701 Frederick crowned himself King in Prussia at Königsberg, becoming Frederick I. The title said ‘in’ rather than ‘of’ Prussia because <b>West Prussia</b> was still Polish, but it gradually came to name the whole Hohenzollern state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the first and second partitions of Poland, in 1772 and 1793, the Kingdom of Prussia, the successor state of the Teutonic Order, annexed the former territory of Royal Prussia and reorganised it as the province of <b>West Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
